EXT. SNOWBOUND FERRY LANDING - 1840s - DUSK

A frozen river, a sagging dock, a single lantern swaying. Wind drives powder across the ice. A barge hull is locked in the freeze like a fly in amber.

FERRYMAN ODO BRANDYWINE (50s, sealskin hat) pokes the ice with a long pole. Beside him a gaunt STRANGER (30s, black greatcoat, scholar's satchel) watches the far shore.

                    ODO
          (to the empty landing)
          Nobody's crossing tonight!

                    STRANGER
          I paid for a crossing.

                    ODO
          You paid for a ferry. This is a skating rink.

                    STRANGER
          Then I will walk.

                    ODO
          You'll die.

                    STRANGER
          Everyone does.

Odo spits. He jabs the pole at the ice, and it cracks like a pistol. They both flinch.

                    ODO
          (muttering)
          Mad as a March hare.

A woman's cry carries across the river. Both men turn. On the far bank, a small figure waves a lantern. The Stranger sets down his satchel and shouts.

                    STRANGER
          Wilhelmina!

                    ODO
          You know her?

                    WILHELMINA (O.S.)
          (distant)
          Thaddeus! The ice is thin!

                    STRANGER
          (calling)
          I'm coming!

                    ODO
          Thaddeus. Is that your name?

                    THADDEUS
          It is.

                    ODO
          (to himself)
          Well, Thaddeus, you are about to be a dead one.

Thaddeus picks up his satchel and steps onto the ice. Odo grabs his coat.

                    ODO (CONT'D)
          Wait! At least take the pole!

Thaddeus takes it. He tests the ice with the pole. It holds. Another step. Another. The lantern on the far bank swings in time with his footsteps.

                    ODO (CONT'D)
          (shouting after him)
          If you see the river open, do not stop for love!

                    THADDEUS
          (over his shoulder)
          I never have.

Odo watches him shrink to a speck against the blue snow. The ice groans. A long crack races beneath the ferry.

                    ODO
          (quietly)
          God keep you, you fool.
